(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_gravitation=[{id:"neet-2026r-q015",pn:15,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Escape speed",q:"Two planets P\u2081 and P\u2082 with equal mass have radii R\u2081 and R\u2082, respectively, where R\u2082 = R\u2081/2. The escape speeds of P\u2081 and P\u2082 are v\u2081 and v\u2082, respectively. Then v\u2082/v\u2081 is:",o:["1","\u221A2","2","1/\u221A2"],c:1,s:`Escape speed v_e = \u221A(2gR) = \u221A(2GM/R).
M is the same for both, so v_e \u221D 1/\u221AR.
v\u2082/v\u2081 = \u221A(R\u2081/R\u2082) = \u221A2.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q026",pn:26,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Kepler's law of periods",q:"In a solar system, the time-period of revolution of a planet tracing a circular orbit of radius R is proportional to:",o:["R^(3/2)","R\xB2","R\xB3","R^(1/2)"],c:0,s:`In planetary motion, T\xB2 \u221D R\xB3.
So T \u221D R^(3/2).`},{id:"neet-2026-q027",pn:27,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Gravitational potential energy",q:"The amount of work done to raise a mass m from the surface of the Earth to a height equal to the radius of the Earth R will be",o:["2mgR","mgR","mgR/4","mgR/2"],c:3,s:`Work done = U\u2082 \u2212 U\u2081.
At height R the distance from the centre is 2R, so U\u2082 = \u2212GMm/(R + R) = \u2212GMm/(2R).
U\u2081 = \u2212GMm/R.
W = \u2212GMm/(2R) + GMm/R = GMm/(2R).
Using GM = gR\xB2, W = mgR/2.`},{id:"neet-2025-q031",pn:31,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Kepler's laws",q:"The radius of Martian orbit around the Sun is about 4 times the radius of the orbit of Mercury. The Martian year is 687 Earth days. Then which of the following is the length of 1 year on Mercury?",o:["88 earth days","225 earth days","172 earth days","124 earth days"],c:0,s:`Apply Kepler\u2019s 3rd law: T\xB2 \u221D R\xB3.
Radius of Martian orbit R\u2032 = 4R.
(T\u2032/T)\xB2 = (R\u2032/R)\xB3 = (4R/R)\xB3 = 4\xB3 = 64.
So T\u2032/T = 8.
Length of 1 year on Mercury T = T\u2032/8 = 687/8 = 85.88 days.
Note: printed answer (1) 88 days; the working gives 85.88 days, which is closest to 88 (Mercury's year is about 88 days)`},{id:"neet-2025-q032",pn:32,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Variation of g with height",q:"A body weighs 48 N on the surface of the earth. The gravitational force experienced by the body due to the earth at a height equal to one-third the radius of the earth from its surface is :",o:["16 N","27 N","32 N","36 N"],c:1,s:`W = mg, with g = GM/R\xB2 and g_h = GM/(R+h)\xB2.
W_h/W = mg_h/mg = g_h/g = R\xB2/(R+h)\xB2, with h = R/3.
W_h/W = R\xB2/(R + R/3)\xB2 = R\xB2/(4R/3)\xB2 = 9/16.
W_h = (9/16)W = (9/16) \xD7 48 (W = 48 N).
W_h = 27 N.`},{id:"neet-2024-q024",pn:24,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Acceleration due to gravity",q:"The mass of a planet is (1)/(10)th that of the earth and its diameter is half that of earth. The acceleration due to gravity on that planet is-",o:["4.9 m s\u207B\xB2","3.92 m s\u207B\xB2","19.6 m s\u207B\xB2","9.8 m s\u207B\xB2"],c:1,s:`g\u2032 = GM\u2032/R\u2032\xB2, with M\u2032 = M/10 and R\u2032 = R/2.
g\u2032 = (GM/10)/(R/2)\xB2 = (4/10)(GM/R\xB2).
g\u2032 = 0.4 \xD7 9.8 = 3.92 m s\u207B\xB2.`},{id:"neet-2024-q049",pn:49,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Satellite launch energy",q:"The minimum energy required to launch a satellite of mass m from the surface of earth of mass M and radius R in a circular orbit at an altitude of 2R from the surface of the earth is:",o:["GmM/(2R)","GmM/(3R)","5GmM/(6R)","2GmM/(3R)"],c:2,s:`Use energy conservation: U_i + K_i = U_f + K_f.
Initially U_i = \u2212GMm/R. At altitude 2R the orbit radius is 3R, so U_f = \u2212GMm/(3R).
Orbital speed at 3R satisfies v\xB2 = GM/(3R), so K_f = \xBD m \xD7 GM/(3R).
\u2212GMm/R + K_i = \u2212GMm/(3R) + \xBD \xD7 m \xD7 GM/(3R).
K_i = \u2212(1/6)(GMm/R) + GMm/R.
K_i = (5/6) GMm/R.`},{id:"neet-2023-q010",pn:10,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Gravitational potential and field",q:"Two bodies of mass m and 9 m are placed at a distance R. The gravitational potential on the line joining the bodies where the gravitational field equals zero, will be (G = gravitational constant):",o:["\u221220 Gm/R","\u22128 Gm/R","\u221212 Gm/R","\u221216 Gm/R"],c:3,s:`Let the zero-field point Q be at distance x from mass m, so R \u2212 x from mass 9m.
Fields cancel: Gm/x\xB2 = G(9m)/(R \u2212 x)\xB2.
So (R \u2212 x)\xB2/x\xB2 = 9, which gives x = R/4.
Potential at Q = \u2212Gm/x \u2212 G(9m)/(R \u2212 x).
= \u2212Gm/(R/4) \u2212 9Gm/(3R/4) = \u22124Gm/R \u2212 12Gm/R = \u221216Gm/R.`,sfig:{w:300,h:110,alt:"Line of length R: mass m at the left end, 9m at the right end, point Q at distance x from m and R \u2212 x from 9m.",d:[["l",150,22,30,22,"a"],["l",150,22,270,22,"a"],["t",150,16,"R","m"],["l",30,60,270,60],["c",30,60,3,"F"],["c",90,60,3,"F"],["c",270,60,3,"F"],["t",30,50,"m","m"],["t",270,50,"9m","m"],["t",90,88,"Q","m"],["l",60,76,32,76,"a"],["l",60,76,88,76,"a"],["t",60,72,"x","m"],["l",180,76,92,76,"a"],["l",180,76,268,76,"a"],["l",60,76,32,76,"a"],["l",60,76,88,76,"a"],["t",60,72,"x","m"],["l",180,76,92,76,"a"],["l",180,76,268,76,"a"],["t",180,72,"(R \u2212 x)","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2023-q045",pn:45,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Time period of near-earth satellite",q:"A satellite is orbiting just above the surface of the earth with period T. If d is the density of the earth and G is the universal constant of gravitation, the quantity 3\u03C0/(Gd) represents :",o:["\u221AT","T","T\xB2","T\xB3"],c:2,s:`For a satellite just above the surface, T = 2\u03C0\u221A(R\xB3/GM).
M = (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3d, so T = 2\u03C0\u221A(3/(4\u03C0Gd)).
Squaring, T\xB2 = 4\u03C0\xB2 \xD7 3/(4\u03C0Gd) = 3\u03C0/(Gd).
So the quantity is T\xB2.`},{id:"neet-2022-q003",pn:3,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Gravitational field intensity",q:"A body of mass 60 g experiences a gravitational force of 3.0 N, when placed at a particular point. The magnitude of the gravitational field intensity at that point is",o:["50 N/kg","20 N/kg","180 N/kg","0.05 N/kg"],c:0,s:`Gravitational field intensity is force per unit mass, F = mE.
Mass = 60 g = 60/1000 kg.
3 = (60/1000) \xD7 E, so E = 50 N/kg.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q033",pn:33,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Conservative gravitational force",q:"A gravitational field is present in a region and a mass is shifted from A to B through different paths as shows. If W\u2081 W\u2082 and W\u2083 represent the work done by the gravitational force along the respective paths, then:",o:["W\u2081 < W\u2082 < W\u2083","W\u2081 = W\u2082 = W\u2083","W\u2081 > W\u2082 > W\u2083","W\u2081 > W\u2083 > W\u2082"],c:1,s:`Gravitational force is a conservative force.
Work done by a conservative force does not depend on the path.
So W\u2081 = W\u2082 = W\u2083.`,fig:{w:230,h:145,alt:"Points A and B in a gravitational field joined by three different paths 1, 2 and 3.",d:[["t",13,104,"A","e"],["t",206,104,"B"],["p","M20 100V25H200V100"],["l",100,25,112,25,"a"],["t",110,16,"1","m"],["p","M20 100C45 76 75 80 105 94C140 110 175 106 200 100"],["p","M55 82.9L66 82.7","a"],["t",70,70,"2","m"],["p","M20 100C55 118 95 120 130 114C165 108 185 104 200 100"],["p","M69 115.2L81 116.8","a"],["t",110,136,"3","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2022r-q036",pn:36,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Field from potential",q:"In a gravitational field, the gravitational potential is given by, V = \u2212K/x (J/kg). The gravitational field intensity at point (2, 0, 3)m is:",o:["+K/4","+K/2","\u2212K/2","\u2212K/4"],c:3,s:`Field E = \u2212dV/dx.
E = \u2212d(\u2212K/x)/dx = \u2212K/x\xB2 (along \xEE).
At x = 2 m, E = \u2212K/4.`},{id:"neet-2022-q039",pn:39,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Dimensions of gravitational quantities",q:"Match List-I with List-II. List-I: (a) Gravitational constant (G) (b) Gravitational potential energy (c) Gravitational potential (d) Gravitational intensity. List-II: (i) [L\xB2T\u207B\xB2] (ii) [M\u207B\xB9L\xB3T\u207B\xB2] (iii) [LT\u207B\xB2] (iv) [ML\xB2T\u207B\xB2]. Choose the correct answer from the options given below",o:["(a) - (ii), (b) - (iv), (c) - (i), (d) - (iii)","(a) - (ii), (b) - (iv), (c) - (iii), (d) - (i)","(a) - (iv), (b) - (ii), (c) - (i), (d) - (iii)","(a) - (ii), (b) - (i), (c) - (iv), (d) - (iii)"],c:0,s:`(a) G = Fr\xB2/(m\u2081m\u2082) = [MLT\u207B\xB2][L\xB2]/[M\xB2] = [M\u207B\xB9L\xB3T\u207B\xB2].
(b) Potential energy has dimensions [ML\xB2T\u207B\xB2].
(c) Potential = energy/mass = [L\xB2T\u207B\xB2].
(d) Intensity = force/mass = [LT\u207B\xB2].
So (a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii).`},{id:"neet-2021-q013",pn:13,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Escape velocity",q:"The escape velocity from the Earth's surface is v. The escape velocity from the surface of another planet having a radius, four times that of Earth and same mass density is",o:["4v","v","2v","3v"],c:0,s:`ve = \u221A(2GM/R) with M = \u03C1(4/3)\u03C0R\xB3.
ve = \u221A(2G\xB7\u03C1(4/3)\u03C0R\xB3/R) = \u221A((8/3)G\u03C1\u03C0 R\xB2) = R\u221A((8/3)G\u03C1\u03C0).
For the same density, ve \u221D R.
R is 4 times larger, so the escape velocity is 4v.`},{id:"neet-2021-q038",pn:38,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Maximum height under gravity",q:"A particle of mass 'm' is projected with a velocity v = kVe (k < 1) from the surface of the earth. (Ve = escape velocity) The maximum height above the surface reached by the particle is",o:["Rk\xB2/(1 \u2013 k\xB2)","R(k/(1 \u2013 k))\xB2","R(k/(1 + k))\xB2","R\xB2k/(1 + k)"],c:0,s:`Energy conservation from the surface to the top: (1)/(2)mv\xB2 \u2013 GMm/R = \u2013GMm/(R + h).
So (1)/(2)v\xB2 = GM/R \u2013 GM/(R + h) = GMh/(R(R + h)).
Ve\xB2 = 2GM/R, so v\xB2 = k\xB2\xB72GM/R.
k\xB2\xB7GM/R = GMh/(R(R + h)), so k\xB2 = h/(R + h).
k\xB2R + k\xB2h = h, so h(1 \u2013 k\xB2) = Rk\xB2.
h = Rk\xB2/(1 \u2013 k\xB2).`},{id:"neet-2020-q115",pn:115,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Variation of g with height",q:"A body weighs 72 N on the surface of the earth. What is the gravitational force on it, at a height equal to half the radius of the earth ?",o:["32 N","30 N","24 N","48 N"],c:0,s:`Weight at height h = W/(1 + h/R)\xB2.
h = R/2, so (1 + 1/2)\xB2 = 9/4.
W_h = 72/(9/4) = 72 \xD7 4/9 = 32 N.`},{id:"neet-2019-q109",pn:109,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Variation of g with depth",q:"A body weighs 200 N on the surface of the earth. How much will it weigh half way down to the centre of the earth?",o:["250 N","100 N","150 N","200 N"],c:1,s:`Inside the earth g\u2032 = g\xB7r/R.
At half the radius r = R/2, so g\u2032 = g/2.
Weight = 200/2 = 100 N.`},{id:"neet-2019-q118",pn:118,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Gravitational potential energy",q:"The work done to raise a mass m from the surface of the earth to a height h, which is equal to the radius of the earth, is :",o:["\xBD mgR","3/2 mgR","mgR","2 mgR"],c:0,s:`Work = increase in potential energy = GMm/R \u2212 GMm/(2R).
= GMm/(2R).
Using GM = gR\xB2, this is mgR/2.`},{id:"neet-2018-q042",pn:42,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Acceleration due to gravity",q:"If the mass of the Sun were ten times smaller and the universal gravitational constant were ten times larger in magnitude, which of the following is not correct ?",o:["Raindrops will fall faster","Walking on the ground would become more difficult","Time period of a simple pendulum on the Earth would decrease","'g' on the Earth will not change"],c:3,s:`g on Earth = (GMe)/(R\xB2) depends on G and Earth's mass, not on the Sun's mass.
G becomes 10 times larger, so g becomes 10 times larger.
Raindrops then fall faster and walking is harder, and T = 2\u03C0\u221A(l/g) decreases.
So statement (4) is the incorrect one.`},{id:"neet-2018-q044",pn:44,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Kepler's laws and orbital speed",q:"The kinetic energies of a planet in an elliptical orbit about the Sun, at positions A, B and C are KA, KB and KC, respectively. AC is the major axis and SB is perpendicular to AC at the position of the Sun S as shown in the figure. Then",o:["KA < KB < KC","KA > KB > KC","KB < KA < KC","KB > KA > KC"],c:1,s:`A is the nearest point to the Sun and C is the farthest, so rA < rB < rC.
Angular momentum mvr is conserved, so speed is larger where r is smaller: vA > vB > vC.
Kinetic energy follows speed: KA > KB > KC.`,fig:{w:290,h:125,alt:"Ellipse with major axis AC, the Sun S on the axis nearer A, and SB perpendicular to AC with B on the ellipse.",d:[["p","M30 70A110 45 0 1 1 250 70A110 45 0 1 1 30 70Z",""],["l",30,70,250,70,""],["l",85,70,85,31,""],["c",85,70,2.6,"F"],["c",85,70,3.8,"F"],["p","M91 70V64H85",""],["t",24,74,"A","e"],["t",256,74,"C",""],["t",90,88,"S",""],["t",85,22,"B","m"]]}}]})();
